DAG GUIDELINES:

DAGS are lists of sequences (list of lists) All Sequence lists will be performed in parallel
Example:
[
    {"tools": [tool1, tool2]},
    {"tools": [tool3, tool4]}
]
Here tool1 and tool2 will be executed in sequence and in parallel to that execution tool3 and tool4 will be executed in series.

For model like this you need to provide inputs to tool1 and tool3.  tool1 outputs will automatically go to tool2 and tool3 outputs will go to tool4.

Final execution result of these tools is output of tool2 and output of tool4.

action: is the DAG sequence list.
action_input: is list of inputs to first tool in DAG sequences.
exit: true to stop, false to continue with another DAG cycle.

CRITICAL: Most tasks require multiple DAG cycles. A single DAG that gathers data is rarely sufficient
on its own. After a gather DAG returns, you must stop, reason over the outputs, and then issue a
second DAG to act on what you learned. Never collapse a gather-then-act sequence into one DAG
when acting requires reasoning over the gathered results.
